# SRG-APP-000174-AU-002570  -  CAT III
# The Central Log Server must be configured to enforce a 180-day maximum
# password lifetime restriction.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000174-AU-002570
group:           V-206477
severity:        low
cat:             CAT III
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-004066
control_rev5:    IA-5

contribution:    Splunk's native authentication can turn password expiry on and set the
                 number of days after which a password must be changed, which is the whole
                 of the maximum password lifetime for accounts the application holds.

governs:         authentication.conf - How users sign in, and the password rules applied to local accounts.

check:           authentication.conf [splunk_auth] expireUserAccounts in true|1
check:           authentication.conf [splunk_auth] expirePasswordDays max 180
check:           authentication.conf [splunk_auth] expirePasswordDays min 1
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
corrected:       authentication.conf [splunk_auth] expireUserAccounts equals true -> authentication.conf [splunk_auth] expireUserAccounts in true|1
vendor:          authentication.conf [splunk_auth] expirePasswordDays https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/authentication.conf
vendor:          authentication.conf [splunk_auth] expireUserAccounts https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/authentication.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Where authType is LDAP or SAML, password lifetime for federated accounts
                 is enforced by the directory service or identity provider; [splunk_auth]
                 governs the native accounts that remain, including the emergency account
                 of last resort
external:        Operating system password aging for host accounts

citation:        SPLK-CL-000400 check text ("If the expirePasswordDays is missing or is
                 configured to 61 or more, this is a finding") and fix text
                 ("expirePasswordDays = 60"); authentication.conf.spec:487-494
                 (expirePasswordDays, minimum 0, maximum 3650, default 90),
                 authentication.conf.spec:504-508 (expireUserAccounts, boolean, default
                 false, meaning passwords do not expire)
finding:         authentication.conf [splunk_auth] does not enable password expiry
                 (expireUserAccounts is not true) or expirePasswordDays exceeds 180 days,
                 so the application does not enforce a maximum password lifetime on the
                 accounts it holds.
